WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.334
-There are more than
10 million species

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:05.567
of plant and animals
alive today.

00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:13.000
♪♪

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.000
And, yet, they are just 1%

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.567
of all species
that have ever existed.

00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:23.667
♪♪

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:26.601
By traveling back in time,

00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:30.067
we'll meet many of the 99%
that have gone extinct.

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.234
[ Animal growls softly ]

00:00:32.234 --> 00:00:33.400
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.534
We're here with Morgan Freeman,
everybody!

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:36.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:41.367
That was a clip
from "Life on Our Planet."

00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:42.601
This shows us --
-Great show.

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:44.367
It's a wonderful show.
It makes you appreciate

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:46.767
being alive when you see
how this world of ours

00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:48.567
has developed
over billions of years.

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.501
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:53.534
And if we're not careful...
-It does.

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:55.234
You know, it sort of
is a nice thing to watch

00:00:55.234 --> 00:00:57.167
where you realize
that climate change

00:00:57.167 --> 00:00:58.501
could take this all
away from us.

00:00:58.501 --> 00:01:00.067
Is that something
when you get involved

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:01.968
with a project like this --
do you enjoy that

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:03.667
it's not just
the wonder of the world...

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:05.834
-Oh. Absolutely.
Absolutely, Seth.

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:07.734
Um...

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:12.067
We're in such a...

00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:13.968
flux here

00:01:13.968 --> 00:01:19.534
in terms of our existence
on this planet.

00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:25.868
Let's not think that
we're here to stay just because.

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:27.701
We can see.

00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:33.167
If 99% of all life
that has ever lived is gone...

00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:35.534
and only 1% is left...

00:01:35.534 --> 00:01:37.634
we ought to think about that.

00:01:37.634 --> 00:01:40.968
It's like...
well, this planet does things.

00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:42.534
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:45.133
It's like --
And there's no place to go.

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:47.033
-That's true.
-Absolutely no place to go.

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:49.133
So we better be careful!

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:51.567
-That's a good --
That's a good warning.

00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:54.901
You know, in the clip,
it showed, you know,

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:56.367
dinosaurs, prehistoric life,

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:58.634
and you have to do
a narration about that.

00:01:58.634 --> 00:02:00.467
And I'm wondering --
I thought I knew

00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:02.033
how to say
all the dinosaurs' names,

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:04.000
but then I have kids, and they
have their dinosaur books,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.501
and I realize I only know
sort of the top three.

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:07.601
-Yeah, me too.

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:09.501
-And then they get
pretty tricky.

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:11.033
Is that something --
Do you get a --

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:12.901
I mean, I'm assuming
you get a bunch of takes,

00:02:12.901 --> 00:02:16.133
but are you good with those
words you've never seen before?

00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:17.534
-Let me not brag about

00:02:17.534 --> 00:02:19.567
what I shouldn't be
trying to brag about.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:22.767
-You are --
You are a humble man.

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:25.667
And you are also --
We were talking backstage.

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:29.567
You live in the country now.
You live on the Gulf Coast.

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:33.634
-Yes.
Well, it's not "the country."

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:37.067
-Oh.
I didn't mean it to be rude.

00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:39.300
-Well --
-You have stores and stuff?

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.434
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.434 --> 00:02:43.801
-Restaurants.

00:02:43.801 --> 00:02:45.000
-Restaurants?!
-Yeah.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.601
-Okay. I was thinking
of a different thing.

00:02:46.601 --> 00:02:48.534
-Yeah. Right.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.868
But I have a home
in Mississippi also.

00:02:50.868 --> 00:02:52.267
-Gotcha.

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:55.033
-Pretty much where --
I grew up in Mississippi.

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.868
Raise there.
And so I live there.

00:02:57.868 --> 00:03:01.000
But it's just a farm,
and it's --

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.267
That's the country.
-That's the country.

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:04.534
When did it dawn on you?

00:03:04.534 --> 00:03:06.501
Did you think
through your whole life

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:09.033
that at one point you would
find your way back there?

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:10.968
Or was it as you --
as you got older, you thought,

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:12.634
"Oh, you know what,
I like that."

00:03:12.634 --> 00:03:18.534
-No, it was not until
about 1983.

00:03:18.534 --> 00:03:21.467
My parents had moved back.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:25.067
And I started visiting them

00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:28.133
maybe once a year or so.

00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:32.334
And every time I went there,
I was like, "Oh.

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:34.567
This is so relaxing.
This is so nice.

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:35.934
This is...'

00:03:35.934 --> 00:03:40.934
So by 1989 or '90...

00:03:40.934 --> 00:03:43.634
we decided, alright,
we'll build here.

00:03:43.634 --> 00:03:46.300
-Are people happy to see you
back home in Mississippi?

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.734
-Always. Yeah.
-Yeah, I would hope so.

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:50.300
At, like, the stores
and the restaurants?

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.601
-See that?
-What is that? S-I-P?

00:03:52.601 --> 00:03:54.534
-That's The Sip.
-What's The Sip?

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:56.667
-That's --
-Oh, that's Mississippi!

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:58.267
-Yeah! Yo!

00:03:58.267 --> 00:04:01.100
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.767
-Now...

00:04:03.767 --> 00:04:05.934
I just want you to know I would
never have the confidence

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:08.000
to say to you,
"How's The Sip? Is it good?

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.334
Are things in The Sip good?"
But now I'm gonna --

00:04:10.334 --> 00:04:11.834
Next time you come here,
I'm gonna say it.

00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:14.033
-Okay. We're moving right along.

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:15.801
-Alright. I'm glad.
-Yeah.

00:04:15.801 --> 00:04:18.200
Moving all the way
into the 20th century.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.400
-There we go.
-Yeah.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.767
It's certainly easier to spell.
Um...

00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:25.801
I want to ask about another
documentary you produced

00:04:25.801 --> 00:04:27.667
about --

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:29.167
It was -- What was it called?
"The 761st..."

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:30.934
-It was -- Yeah.

00:04:30.934 --> 00:04:34.167
The 761st Tank Battalion.
The Black Panthers.

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:40.868
This was the first
armored battalion, Black,

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:46.367
to be engaged in the war --
World War II.

00:04:46.367 --> 00:04:48.734
Um...Patton.

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:51.601
-And did you know much
about them?

00:04:51.601 --> 00:04:53.367
I know you served
in the Air Force.

00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:55.400
Was this something
you'd ever heard about?

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.767
-No. I had -- A guy came to us.

00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:01.634
I have a company.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.934
You know, we make movies.
-You produce stuff.

00:05:03.934 --> 00:05:07.634
-Produce stuff, you know?
Revelations Entertainment.

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:09.901
And the guy came to us
with this project.

00:05:09.901 --> 00:05:15.000
It was a script about
the 761st Tank Battalion.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.534
I did not know anything
about that.

00:05:17.534 --> 00:05:20.634
So, of course, you can Google it

00:05:20.634 --> 00:05:25.400
and find out everything
you need to know now.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.734
Um, so then -- I don't know.

00:05:28.734 --> 00:05:33.634
But this goes back
to 20, 25 years maybe...

00:05:33.634 --> 00:05:36.133
-Okay.
-...that we've had this project.

00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:40.601
So it's like...
nothing good comes easy.

00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:43.834
And then -- I don't know.

00:05:43.834 --> 00:05:47.067
As things developed,
we were able to make

00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:51.100
a very, very nice documentary
about them.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:54.400
But I want to make a series
to get them out into...

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.100
-Yeah. Sort of a "Band of
Brothers" situation, right?

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.734
-Exactly.
Exactly what I want to do.

00:05:58.734 --> 00:06:00.634
-I think that's a very
safe pair of hands.

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:02.467
I hope you can get that
for us all to see.

00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:04.334
There's a wonderful book
about it, as well,

00:06:04.334 --> 00:06:06.067
called "Come Out Fighting."

00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:08.000
I'm assuming
this is one of these...

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.334
-Yeah, we used that.

00:06:09.334 --> 00:06:11.834
That's part of our background,
our research.

00:06:11.834 --> 00:06:13.267
-There you go.
-Yeah.

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:15.567
-Alright. Well, it's just
a joy to have you back.

00:06:15.567 --> 00:06:16.834
Thank you so much.
What a pleasure.

00:06:16.834 --> 00:06:18.267
-It's a joy to sit here
and talk to you.

00:06:18.267 --> 00:06:20.033
-Morgan Freeman, everybody.

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:22.667
"Life on Our Planet"
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:24.801
We'll be right back
with McKay Coppins.

